1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:06,624
Nigeria Today: five stories people are talking about, explained in about two minutes.

2
00:00:07,374 --> 00:00:09,103
First, Ebola.

3
00:00:09,153 --> 00:00:19,992
Nigeria's only confirmed case this year was a traveller from Uganda, confirmed in Abuja on 20 October and later evacuated to Switzerland.

4
00:00:20,042 --> 00:00:25,846
Two suspected cases in Adamawa tested negative after two rounds of testing.

5
00:00:25,896 --> 00:00:36,095
NCDC says there is no active case. 62 contacts are being monitored, with the last expected to finish on 13 November.

6
00:00:36,145 --> 00:00:45,170
If you have fever, bleeding or severe weakness after possible exposure, seek care, and call NCDC on 6232.

7
00:00:45,920 --> 00:00:48,375
Second, the voter register.

8
00:00:48,425 --> 00:00:58,517
INEC says some registration officials abused their access to create fake records, including photos that appear to be AI-generated.

9
00:00:58,567 --> 00:01:07,677
Its ICT director says about 1.5 million of the 11 million new registrations were flagged as suspicious.

10
00:01:07,727 --> 00:01:16,945
The rights group SERAP wants an independent probe. INEC says the register will be cleaned up before it is certified on 15 December.

11
00:01:17,695 --> 00:01:21,301
Third, the ₦70,000 transport allowance.

12
00:01:21,351 --> 00:01:31,827
The federal government agreed with the NLC to pay public servants ₦70,000 a month for six months, starting this November.

13
00:01:31,877 --> 00:01:41,607
But the TUC rejected it, saying workers need a real pay rise, not a temporary allowance. Labour is now openly divided.

14
00:01:42,357 --> 00:01:43,958
Fourth, borrowing.

15
00:01:44,008 --> 00:01:52,543
The government has sent a five-year external borrowing plan of up to $21.7 billion to the National Assembly.

16
00:01:52,593 --> 00:02:02,450
The finance minister says about $10.6 billion is for transport, including coastal highways and railways, and that not all of it may be drawn.

17
00:02:03,200 --> 00:02:05,633
And finally, some good news.

18
00:02:05,683 --> 00:02:15,306
The Flamingos beat Venezuela 1–0 with an 89th-minute penalty to reach the semi-finals of the U-17 Women's World Cup.

19
00:02:15,356 --> 00:02:21,885
They face Brazil on Wednesday 4 November in Rabat, chasing their first-ever final.

20
00:02:22,635 --> 00:02:27,650
That's Nigeria Today. Check facts with official sources before you share.

